sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: To inscribe a circle in a given equilateral and equiangular pentagon.
----
proof:

Let {ABCDE} be the given equilateral and equiangular pentagon.

It is required to inscribe a circle in the pentagon {ABCDE}.

Bisect the angles {BCD} and {CDE} by the straight lines {CF|FC} and {DF|FD} respectively.  Join the straight lines {FB}, {FA}, and {FE} from the point {F} at which the straight lines {CF|FC} and {DF|FD} meet one another. [!just I.9]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="320" height="310" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="IV.13 diagram" src="propIV13.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // The two bisectors meet at F; the I.4 pair BCF / DCF carries the
    // bisection on to B ("similarly" to A and E); the perpendiculars FG..FM
    // and the I.26 pair FHC / FKC make them equal; the circle on them
    // touches the five sides (III.16) and is described last.
    //
    // Joyce's figure draws the five lines AK, BL, CM, DG, EH, each running
    // from a vertex through F to the foot on the opposite side, and his CF /
    // DF are the hidden angleBisector lines that stop short of F. The proof
    // works with the pieces either side of F, so FC, FD (drawn from C and D,
    // as bisected), FB, FA, FE (joined from F) and the perpendiculars FG..FM
    // are solid black slide twins over Joyce's lines, hidden at rest
    // (initiallyHidden), as on IV.3. The prose reaches the bisectors as
    // {CF|FC} / {DF|FD}, since CF and DF belong to the original elements.
    //
    // Everything else added is transparent: the five sides, the triangles
    // BCF, DCF, FHC, FKC (explicit ;0 faces), and circT, a faceless twin of
    // the circle that the argument talks about before "Let it be described".
    // Angle markers: halves pinned at 20 and wholes at 32 so they lie side by
    // side and nest as drawn; the right angles at G, H, K, L, M are red
    // squares. The angle and triangle names collide (BCF, DCF, FHC, FKC):
    // the triangles own them, the angles take the display override.
    var GIVEN = ["CDEAB","C","D","E","A","B","AB","BC","CD","DE","EA"];
    var BIS   = GIVEN.concat(["angBCD","angCDE","FC","FD"]);
    var JOIN  = GIVEN.concat(["FC","FD","F","FB","FA","FE"]);
    var TRI   = JOIN.concat(["BCF","DCF","angBCF","angDCF"]);
    var OPP   = TRI.concat(["angCBF","angCDF"]);
    var DBL   = OPP.concat(["angCDE"]);
    var ABCv  = DBL.concat(["angABC"]);
    var HALF  = ABCv.concat(["angABF"]);
    var SIM   = JOIN.concat(["angBAE","angAED"]);
    var SIM2  = SIM.concat(["angBAF","angFAE","angAEF","angFED"]);
    var PERP  = JOIN.concat(["FG","FH","FK","FL","FM","G","H","K","L","M"]);
    var RT    = PERP.concat(["rtG","rtH","rtK","rtL","rtM"]);
    var EQ    = RT.concat(["angBCF","angDCF","FHC","FKC"]);
    var HYP   = RT.concat(["circT"]);
    // The right-angle squares are dropped once the circle is described:
    // its opaque face would cover them, and Joyce's figure has none.
    var DONE  = PERP.concat(["circ"]);
